---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Nav from '../../components/Nav.astro';
import { projects } from '../../data/projects';

export function getStaticPaths() {
	return projects.map((project) => ({
		params: { slug: project.slug },
		props: { project },
	}));
}

const { project } = Astro.props;
---

<Layout title={`${project.title} — Hiro Eernisse`} description={project.blurb}>
	<Nav />

	<main class="mx-auto max-w-[960px] px-5 py-12">
		{/* No back link up here on purpose. It used to be that the sticky nav's
		    Projects link never left the viewport, so a second one this close read
		    as two of the same control; that link is gone (08-01), and the reason
		    is now simply that the footer's does the job better — it returns you
		    to the card you came from, at the scroll position you left it. */}

		{/* Dateline is the year alone — the company already reads off the prose
		    below, and tags/Visit were dropped from this page (both still live in
		    projects.ts).

		    THE SYNTAX ACCENTS ARE GONE FROM THIS HEADER (2026-08-06, Hiro: match
		    the about page's colours). The year wore --ink-num's orange and the
		    title wore --ink-title's cyan; the about page runs one ink family and
		    no accents — --ink-heading for headings, plain --ink for prose,
		    --ink-soft for the quiet line — and this header now does the same.

		    What it costs, so nobody rediscovers it: the title's cyan was an echo
		    of the card you arrived from, whose h3 still carries --ink-title (see
		    ProjectCard.astro). That thread is cut. The card's YEAR, note, already
		    went plain ink on 08-02 for the same reason in miniature — two accents
		    in one small block and the orange won the entrance — so the two headers
		    disagreed on the year long before they disagreed on the title.
		    --ink-num now has no consumer left on the site. */}
		<header class="reveal" style="--i: 0">
			<p class="text-xs tabular-nums text-[var(--ink-soft)]">{project.year}</p>
			{/* 1.7rem (32.3px), hand-set. Two cuts in one day, and the second is
			    the one that needs explaining.

			    First the sm:text-4xl bump went: it crossed the line the about
			    page's h1 draws in its own note — 4xl is "still the line not to
			    cross" — and this page was the one crossing it. That left text-3xl
			    (35.6px), equal to about's h1.

			    Then Hiro called it slightly too big anyway, so this is now a
			    hand-set value BETWEEN the rungs rather than a scale step. Off
			    3xl by 9%, which is the "slightly" — text-2xl was the alternative
			    and drops 20%, landing nearer about's h2 (23.8px) than its h1.

			    So the two pages' titles no longer match exactly, and that is
			    deliberate: about's h1 is a GREETING sized by the argument in its
			    own note (a greeting is not a page title), while this one is an
			    actual page title carrying a real project name — the longest
			    strings on the site, which is what made 35.6px feel large here and
			    not there. Same tier, not the same number. */}
			<h1 class="mt-2 text-[1.7rem] [font-weight:var(--weight-emph)] tracking-tight text-[var(--ink-heading)]">{project.title}</h1>
		</header>

		{/* Reading matter is capped at --measure (see global.css) — ~11 words a
		    line — while the page itself spans the nav's full 960px, which the
		    images below fill.

		    No "Background" / "Work" headings: two paragraphs in one screen give
		    a reader nothing to skip to, and the labels were the same small-bold
		    kicker shape the home page dropped. The register change they marked
		    — context, then what I did — now rides the type instead. The problem
		    paragraph is set as a lead — a step up in SIZE, and as of 08-06 that
		    is the whole of it; it used to have --ink-soft and a heavier weight
		    doing the same job. See the note in the section below. */}
		<section class="reveal mt-8 max-w-[var(--measure)]" style="--i: 1">
			{/* SIZED AND COLOURED OFF THE ABOUT PAGE (2026-08-06, Hiro: smaller,
			    and match about's colours). The work paragraph is now that page's
			    prose spec exactly — text-sm, plain --ink — and the lead sits one
			    step above it at text-base. They were text-xl / text-lg.

			    The lead lost --ink-soft in the same move, and that is the happy
			    half of it: this paragraph was the site's one RESIDUAL APCA
			    SHORTFALL. The 08-05 AAA pass had lifted --ink-soft to 7.06:1
			    (Lc 42.1 → 48.6), leaving it WCAG-AAA clean in both themes but
			    still short of APCA, which wanted 35px at w400 from that Lc
			    against the 23.75px it was set at — a gap no weight could close,
			    because it was a COLOUR gap. The old note said so and said the fix
			    was to set this paragraph in --ink. Matching about did that.

			    THE WEIGHTS CAME OFF LATER THE SAME DAY (Hiro: body text needs to
			    be lighter). Both paragraphs had carried the UI tier explicitly —
			    w500 light / w400 dark — which was the one weight bump the
			    2026-08-04 legibility audit required back when they were set at
			    23.75/21.375px. They now inherit the body tier (w400 light / w300
			    dark) like every other paragraph on the site.

			    An earlier draft of this note argued the bump was load-bearing and
			    told the next person not to remove it. That argument was about the
			    OLD sizes and it does not survive the move to about's scale, so
			    here is the honest version:

			    The work paragraph is now the about page's prose spec EXACTLY —
			    text-sm, body weight, plain --ink, same leading. Not "close to" it
			    and not a step off it; the same four values. Whatever legibility
			    bar that pairing clears everywhere else on the site, it clears
			    here, because it is the same text set the same way. The lead is
			    that pairing one size LARGER, so it is strictly ahead of it.

			    What genuinely changed is the MARGIN, and that is the thing to
			    know before touching this again: the work paragraph used to sit a
			    weight tier above the site's baseline prose and now sits level
			    with it. It has no headroom left. Anything that lowers this
			    pairing further — a lighter tier, a smaller size, a softer ink —
			    takes the page below a bar the rest of the site holds, and would
			    be the first change here that needs its own APCA number rather
			    than an equivalence to about. Going lighter than the site's
			    lightest is not a taste call any more.

			    ── The lead got half a tier back (Hiro, same day, third pass) ──
			    Size alone turned out to be too little separation once the weights
			    came off, so the lead now wears --weight-lead: 450 light / 350
			    dark, a HALF step above the body rather than the full step it had
			    that morning. The full step was --weight-ui, i.e. precisely where
			    it had started, so taking it would have made the lighten a no-op
			    here and left the ask unanswered.

			    The distinction is back to two channels — size and weight — but
			    the weight channel is now half as loud as it was, and colour (the
			    third, --ink-soft) is not coming back: that one was the APCA
			    shortfall above.

			    The token is a fraction of a tier and can only exist because the
			    face is a continuous variable range; global.css's :root block
			    carries the full argument and the one way it breaks. */}
			<p class="text-base [font-weight:var(--weight-lead)] leading-relaxed">{project.problem}</p>
			<p class="mt-6 text-sm leading-relaxed">{project.work}</p>
		</section>

		<section class="reveal mt-10 space-y-5" style="--i: 2">
			{
				project.images.map((src, i) => (
					<img
						src={src}
						alt={`${project.title} — image ${i + 1}`}
						loading={i === 0 ? 'eager' : 'lazy'}
						class="w-full rounded-[var(--radius)] border border-[var(--line)] bg-[var(--surface)]"
					/>
				))
			}
		</section>
	</main>

	{/* The one back link on the page, and not a duplicate of the nav's
	    Projects: the slug sends you to the CARD you came from at the scroll
	    position you left it (Nav.astro's nav-return / returnY), where the nav
	    link lands on the top of the list. Worth its place down here, several
	    thousand pixels of screenshots below where you started. */}
	<footer class="mx-auto max-w-[960px] px-5 py-10">
		<a
			href={`/#project-${project.slug}`}
			data-projects-link
			data-project-slug={project.slug}
			class="btn-quiet"
		>
			{/* The card's arrow, mirrored — same viewBox and stroke, pointing the
			    other way. Decorative: the label carries the accessible name. */}
			<svg
				class="btn-quiet__arrow h-[0.9rem] w-[0.9rem]"
				viewBox="0 0 24 24"
				fill="none"
				stroke="currentColor"
				stroke-width="2"
				stroke-linecap="round"
				stroke-linejoin="round"
				aria-hidden="true"
			>
				<line x1="20" y1="12" x2="5" y2="12"></line>
				<polyline points="12 5 5 12 12 19"></polyline>
			</svg>
			<span class="btn-quiet__label">Back to projects</span>
		</a>
	</footer>
</Layout>
